CAT = "rbtReduce"; SEG = "SRED"
Q = [
# D.1 Functions of behavior
("Data show a client flicks his fingers near his eyes at similar rates when alone, during play, and during work, and no one responds to it. The most likely function is:",
 "Automatic reinforcement", ["Escape from demands", "Access to attention", "Access to tangible items"],
 "The behavior occurs at the same rate in every condition, including alone, and needs no one's response, which points to automatic reinforcement. Escape would be highest during work."),
("A client yells when his mother takes a phone call, and she hands him her tablet to keep him quiet. On days he already has the tablet when she calls, he does not yell. The likely function is:",
 "Access to the tablet", ["The mother's attention", "Escape from waiting", "Automatic reinforcement"],
 "Yelling produces the tablet and does not occur when the tablet is already available, so the function is tangible. If attention were the function, yelling would still occur while she talks on the phone."),
("In a functional analysis, a client's head hitting is high in the demand condition and near zero in the attention, alone, and play conditions. The most likely function is:",
 "Escape from tasks", ["Access to attention", "Automatic reinforcement", "Access to preferred items"],
 "Behavior that is elevated only in the demand condition, where it ends the task briefly, is most likely maintained by escape. Automatic behavior would also show up in the alone condition."),
("ABC data show a client bites his hand right after being told no about a snack, and staff often then give a smaller snack to calm him. The most likely function is:",
 "Access to food items", ["Escape from instructions", "Attention from staff", "Sensory stimulation"],
 "The antecedent is denied access to food and the consequence is food, so the function is tangible. Staff calming him with a snack delivers the item, not mainly attention."),
("Two clients both throw toys. For one, throwing is followed by the RBT walking over to talk. For the other, throwing ends a cleanup task. What does this show?",
 "Same form, different functions", ["Same function, different forms", "Both throws seek attention", "Both throws are for escape"],
 "Topography (what the behavior looks like) does not tell you function. The first client's throwing produces attention and the second's produces escape, so their plans would differ."),
("A client scratches an itchy rash more when it itches and stops when the itch eases. Scratching happens whether or not anyone is around. This is best described as:",
 "Automatic negative reinforcement", ["Automatic positive reinforcement", "Social negative reinforcement", "Social positive reinforcement"],
 "Scratching removes an unpleasant sensation (negative) and needs no other person (automatic). Automatic positive reinforcement would add a pleasant sensation rather than remove an itch."),
# D.2 Antecedent interventions
("A plan for attention-maintained calling out has the RBT chat with the client every 2 minutes on a timer, whatever she is doing. Calling out drops to near zero. Why does this work?",
 "It weakens the motivating operation", ["It reinforces not calling out", "It adds a cost to calling out", "It teaches a request for attention"],
 "NCR gives attention freely, so the client is no longer deprived of it and calling out loses its value. Reinforcing the absence of calling out would be DRO, which depends on behavior; NCR does not."),
("The RBT gives a client three easy requests he always follows, praising each, before a request he usually refuses. To make this sequence work best, the RBT should:",
 "Ask it right after the last easy one", ["Wait a minute before the hard one", "Give the hard request first", "Hold praise until the hard one"],
 "A high-probability request sequence builds momentum, so the low-probability request should come quickly after the last easy one is reinforced. A pause lets the momentum fade."),
("An escape plan starts the client at 2 worksheet problems per session and adds one problem after each session with no aggression. This procedure is:",
 "Demand fading", ["Stimulus fading", "Graduated guidance", "Prompt fading"],
 "Demand fading starts with few demands and slowly raises them as problem behavior stays low. Stimulus fading changes features of a prompt or stimulus, not the amount of work."),
("A client's elopement from the work table is maintained by access to the playground swings. Which antecedent strategy best fits this function?",
 "Free swing time on a schedule", ["Frequent praise for sitting", "Shorter work tasks at the table", "A reprimand when he stands up"],
 "Scheduled, noncontingent access to the swings reduces the value of eloping to reach them. Shorter tasks would fit escape, praise is a consequence for attention, and a reprimand is not an antecedent change."),
("For escape-maintained screaming during chores, the plan gives a 30-second break every 3 minutes no matter what the client is doing. This procedure is:",
 "Noncontingent escape", ["DRO for screaming", "Escape extinction", "Functional communication training"],
 "Breaks given on a time schedule regardless of behavior are noncontingent escape, a form of NCR. DRO would give the break only if no screaming had occurred."),
# D.3 Differential reinforcement
("In a resetting DRO, a client earns a token if no hitting occurs during each 5-minute interval. At minute 4 of an interval, the client hits. The RBT should:",
 "Restart the 5-minute timer", ["Give the token at minute 5", "Take away an earned token", "End the interval early"],
 "In a resetting DRO, the behavior restarts the interval, and the token comes only after a full interval with no hitting. Taking away a token would be response cost, which is not part of this plan."),
("A client taps the table loudly with her hands. Which replacement would make the plan a DRI rather than a DRA?",
 "Holding a toy with both hands", ["Asking for a short break", "Raising a hand to ask for help", "Keeping both feet on the floor"],
 "DRI reinforces a behavior that cannot occur at the same time as the problem, and holding a toy with both hands makes tapping impossible. Feet on the floor can happen while she taps."),
("A client asks \"Is it lunch yet?\" about 20 times an hour. The goal is fewer questions, not zero. The RBT answers only if 10 minutes have passed since the last question. This is:",
 "Spaced-responding DRL", ["Resetting DRO", "DRA for waiting", "Extinction of questions"],
 "DRL reinforces a behavior only when enough time has passed since the last one, lowering its rate without removing it. DRO would reinforce the absence of questions, aiming for zero."),
("During FCT for escape-maintained crying, the client clearly says \"break please\" as a worksheet is placed in front of her. Early in training, the RBT should:",
 "Give the break right away", ["Finish the worksheet first", "Praise but keep working", "Give the break after a delay"],
 "Early in FCT the new request must work immediately and every time so it competes with crying. Delays and work requirements are added later by the supervisor when thinning the schedule."),
("After FCT, a client asks for a break after every problem. The supervisor changes the plan so breaks are given only after 3 problems are done. This change is:",
 "Schedule thinning", ["Extinction of the request", "Prompt fading", "Response cost"],
 "Gradually requiring more work before the requested break is delivered thins the reinforcement schedule. The request is still reinforced, so it is not extinction."),
("In a DRA plan for attention-maintained whining, a client calmly asks \"Play with me?\" and gets attention, then whines 10 seconds later. The RBT should:",
 "Withhold attention for whining", ["Attend to the whine to calm her", "Tell her to use her calm voice", "Take away one of her tokens"],
 "DRA reinforces the calm request and places the whining on extinction, so the whine gets no attention. A reprimand is still attention and could keep whining going."),
("A momentary DRO checks at the end of each 2-minute interval in a 20-minute session. A sticker is given if the client is not spitting at that moment. He is spitting at 4 checks. He earns:",
 "6 stickers", ["4 stickers", "10 stickers", "16 stickers"],
 "There are 20 / 2 = 10 checks. He was spitting at 4, so he earns 10 - 4 = 6 stickers. Choosing 4 counts the checks with spitting instead of without."),
# D.4 Extinction
("A client screams to get out of brushing his teeth, and the plan calls for escape extinction. He screams during brushing. The RBT should:",
 "Keep guiding him to finish", ["Pause until screaming stops", "Step away and ignore him", "Give a break, then return"],
 "Escape extinction means the screaming no longer ends the task, so brushing continues as the plan describes. Pausing or stepping away lets the task end, which is the reinforcer."),
("A client opens and closes a squeaky door over and over, and the behavior is maintained by the sound. Which is extinction matched to this function?",
 "Oiling the hinge to silence it", ["Ignoring him while he plays", "Taking a token for each swing", "Praising time away from the door"],
 "For automatic reinforcement, extinction removes the sensory consequence, here the squeak. Ignoring would be extinction only if attention were the function."),
("Throwing vegetables is maintained by escape, and the plate is cleared after each throw. The plan now says to ignore the throwing but still clear the plate. Why is this not extinction?",
 "Escape still follows throwing", ["Attention still follows throwing", "Ignoring is a form of punishment", "No replacement is being taught"],
 "Extinction must withhold the reinforcer that maintains the behavior. Clearing the plate still delivers escape, so ignoring changes nothing important. Lack of a replacement is a weakness, but not why this fails as extinction."),
("The RBT ignores a client's yelling for attention all session, but the client's grandmother responds to the yelling at home. The most likely effect is:",
 "Yelling is reinforced intermittently", ["Yelling extinguishes more quickly", "Yelling becomes escape maintained", "Yelling shifts to an automatic function"],
 "Occasional reinforcement from the grandmother puts yelling on an intermittent schedule, which makes it more resistant to extinction. Consistency across settings is needed."),
("At a store checkout, a client cries for a tablet, and the plan uses extinction for this tangible-maintained crying. While the crying continues, the RBT should:",
 "Not give the tablet for crying", ["Give the tablet once crying slows", "Ignore crying but give the tablet", "Give a smaller item instead"],
 "Tangible extinction means crying no longer produces the item. Giving the tablet as crying slows, or a substitute item, still reinforces crying."),
# D.5 Punishment
("Each time a client throws a block, the RBT turns off the cartoon he is watching for 30 seconds while he stays in his seat. This is:",
 "Nonexclusionary time-out", ["Exclusionary time-out", "Response cost for throwing", "Positive punishment"],
 "Access to reinforcement is removed for a set time while the client stays in the area, which is nonexclusionary time-out. Response cost would take away an amount of a reinforcer, such as tokens, rather than access for a period."),
("A teacher asks the RBT to use a brief time-out chair for a client's hitting. Time-out is not in the client's behavior plan. The RBT should:",
 "Decline and tell the supervisor", ["Use it once, then report it", "Use it if the parent agrees", "Try it and record the data"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 2.05 allows punishment-based procedures only when they are in a documented behavior-change plan and the supervisor has verified competence. Parent or teacher agreement does not replace the plan."),
("The supervisor adds response cost to a plan and gives the RBT a written protocol but has not yet watched the RBT run it. Before using it, the RBT should:",
 "Have the supervisor verify skill", ["Use it and take careful data", "Practice it with the client first", "Ask the caregiver to approve it"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 2.05 requires the supervisor to verify competence before an RBT implements a punishment-based procedure, even when it is in the documented plan. Practicing on the client is using it before verification."),
("Time-out sends a client to a quiet corner, but the session area offers hard work with little praise or fun. Why might time-out fail here?",
 "Time-in is not rich enough", ["The time-out is too brief", "The corner is too far away", "It gives too much attention"],
 "Time-out works only if the time-in setting is reinforcing, so losing access to it matters. When time-in is mostly hard work, leaving it may even function as escape."),
("A response cost plan says to remove a token right after each swear, but the RBT sometimes removes tokens at the end of the session instead. The main problem is:",
 "Delayed punishers work poorly", ["The client earns too many tokens", "It becomes negative reinforcement", "It turns into extinction"],
 "Like reinforcement, punishment is most effective when it follows the behavior immediately. Removing tokens later weakens the link to swearing and is not what the plan says."),
# D.6 Secondary effects of extinction and punishment
("During extinction of screaming for attention, a client starts tapping the RBT's arm and saying her name, which she rarely did before. This is:",
 "Response variation", ["Spontaneous recovery", "Resurgence", "Stimulus generalization"],
 "Extinction often produces new or different forms of behavior as the old one stops working. Resurgence would be an older behavior that was once reinforced returning."),
("After a teacher starts reprimanding a client often, the client begins hiding in the bathroom before class and avoids looking at the teacher. This is best described as:",
 "Avoidance of the punisher", ["An extinction burst", "Resurgence of old behavior", "Spontaneous recovery"],
 "Escape and avoidance of the person or setting linked to punishment are common side effects of punishment. An extinction burst is a rise in the behavior placed on extinction."),
("Hitting averaged 8 per session in baseline. On the first 3 days of extinction, counts were 12, 16, and 14. What is the mean for those days, and what does it suggest?",
 "14, an extinction burst", ["12, an extinction burst", "42, an extinction burst", "14, spontaneous recovery"],
 "The mean is (12 + 16 + 14) / 3 = 42 / 3 = 14, well above baseline right after extinction began, which fits a burst. 42 is the sum, not the mean."),
("A client learned to sign \"help\" in place of biting. When a substitute stops honoring the sign for a week, biting returns. This is:",
 "Resurgence of biting", ["Spontaneous recovery", "An extinction burst", "Response generalization"],
 "Resurgence is the return of a previously reinforced behavior when the alternative behavior is put on extinction. Spontaneous recovery is the return of the extinguished behavior itself after time passes."),
("Two weeks after a client's yelling for attention stopped under extinction, it reappears at the start of one session. The RBT should:",
 "Keep withholding attention", ["Attend briefly to calm her", "Stop the plan since it failed", "Add a reprimand for yelling"],
 "A brief return after extinction is spontaneous recovery and usually fades if the plan is followed. Attending to it, even briefly, would reinforce yelling, and a reprimand is not in the plan."),
# D.7 Crisis and emergency procedures
("A client bangs his head hard on a wall at home. The crisis plan says to place a cushion between his head and the wall and call the supervisor. The RBT is trained in a hold not in the plan. The RBT should:",
 "Follow the plan's cushion step", ["Use the trained hold instead", "Treat it as attention extinction", "Wait for the parent to respond"],
 "The RBT follows the crisis plan as written, and RBT Ethics Code 2.05 limits restrictive procedures to those in the documented plan. Training in a hold does not add it to this client's plan."),
("On a community outing, a client with no seizure history and no seizure plan has a seizure. After keeping him safe from injury, the RBT should first:",
 "Call emergency services", ["Call the supervisor for advice", "Hold him still until it ends", "Take duration data, then decide"],
 "A first seizure with no plan is a medical emergency, so emergency services come first, then the supervisor. Holding a person still during a seizure can cause injury."),
("A client's crisis plan lists pacing and clenched fists as early warning signs. The RBT sees both during work. According to the plan, the best time to step in is:",
 "Now, at the early signs", ["Once aggression starts", "After the session ends", "When the client asks for help"],
 "Crisis plans use early signs so staff can de-escalate before behavior becomes dangerous. Waiting for aggression misses the safest point to intervene."),
]
